{{also|Bonnet}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{etyl|enm}} ''[[bonet#Middle English|bonet]]'' from {{etyl|frm}} ''[[bonet#Middle French|bonet]]'' (Modern {{fr}} ''[[bonnet#French|bonnet]]'') from {{etyl|fro}} ''[[bonet#Old French|bonet]]'' "material from which hats are made", of {{etyl|gem}} origin from {{etyl|frk}} *{{term||bunni|that which is bound}} from {{proto|Germanic|bund-|bundle|lang=}}. Compare {{etyl|LL.}} ''[[abbonis]]'', ''[[obbonis]]'' "ribbon of a headdress", also of {{etyl|gem}} origin from {{etyl|frk}} *{{term||obbunni}} from *{{term||ob-|above}} + *{{term||bunni}}. More at [[above]], [[bundle]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/bɒnət/}}
* {{audio|en-us-bonnet.ogg|Audio (US)}}

* {{rhymes|ɒnɪt}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A type of [[hat]], once worn by [[women]] or [[children]], held in place by [[ribbon]]s tied under the [[chin]].
# A traditional Scottish woollen brimless cap, also spelled [[bunnet]].
# {{context|AU|British|automotive}} The [[hinge]]d [[cover]] over the [[engine]] of a [[motor car]]. Also called [[hood]] in other countries.
# {{nautical}} A length of [[canvas]] attached to a [[fore-and-aft]] [[sail]] to increase the pulling power.

====Synonyms====
* (''Scottish cap'') [[tam o'shanter]]
* (''cover over the engine of a motor car''): [[hood]] (''US'')

